1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,559
Every app you use started as an idea in someone's head.

2
00:00:04,029 --> 00:00:07,630
So how does an idea become something a customer can actually use?

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,081
That is the software development life cycle.

4
00:00:10,101 --> 00:00:12,755
In this lesson, you will learn all seven stages.

5
00:00:19,275 --> 00:00:23,564
We will follow one small online shop, called CloudCart, from idea to customer.

6
00:00:24,034 --> 00:00:27,215
By the end, you can name every stage, say what happens in it,

7
00:00:27,235 --> 00:00:28,580
and see where DevOps fits.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:36,905
Stage one: plan. Before anyone writes code, the team agrees on the problem.

9
00:00:36,925 --> 00:00:38,691
Who is it for, and what must it do?

10
00:00:42,161 --> 00:00:46,599
For CloudCart, the goal is simple: customers browse products and place an order.

11
00:00:50,069 --> 00:00:52,519
A written plan avoids building the wrong thing,

12
00:00:52,539 --> 00:00:55,041
which is the most expensive mistake in software.

13
00:00:58,561 --> 00:01:02,914
Stage two: requirements. Now the plan becomes a list of precise statements.

14
00:01:03,384 --> 00:01:07,439
Functional requirements say what the system does, like show a list of products.

15
00:01:10,909 --> 00:01:15,450
Non-functional requirements say how well it does it, like answer within one second.

16
00:01:15,470 --> 00:01:17,758
Vague requirements are why projects drift.

17
00:01:24,278 --> 00:01:27,593
Stage three: design. Here the team decides the parts of the system,

18
00:01:27,613 --> 00:01:29,186
and how they talk to each other.

19
00:01:29,656 --> 00:01:32,588
CloudCart has a browser, an A P I, and a data store.

20
00:01:32,608 --> 00:01:36,335
The browser asks, the A P I answers, and the data store remembers.

21
00:01:42,805 --> 00:01:46,966
Drawing this first lets people spot problems while they are still cheap to fix.

22
00:01:48,486 --> 00:01:51,744
Stage four: build. Developers turn the design into code.

23
00:01:51,764 --> 00:01:53,501
This is real CloudCart source.

24
00:01:53,971 --> 00:01:56,047
This function calculates an order total.

25
00:01:56,067 --> 00:01:59,455
It checks the quantity, finds the product, and adds up the price.

26
00:02:07,925 --> 00:02:11,851
Code lives in version control, so every change is tracked and can be undone.

27
00:02:16,371 --> 00:02:20,746
Stage five: test. Automated tests check the code every time it changes.

28
00:02:21,216 --> 00:02:26,017
This is a real run from our pipeline: eleven tests, zero failures, build success.

29
00:02:29,487 --> 00:02:33,776
If even one test failed, the change would stop here, before any customer sees it.

30
00:02:37,296 --> 00:02:42,140
Stage six: release and deploy. The tested package is placed on a server and started.

31
00:02:42,610 --> 00:02:44,757
Then we check it again while it is running.

32
00:02:44,777 --> 00:02:48,990
The health check answers up, and an order for two items totals seven thousand cents.

33
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:58,643
Passing tests and a working deployment are two different checks. You need both.

34
00:03:03,163 --> 00:03:04,813
Stage seven: operate and learn.

35
00:03:04,833 --> 00:03:08,370
We watch the system, collect feedback, and the cycle starts again.

36
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:11,373
DevOps is about making this loop fast and safe,

37
00:03:11,393 --> 00:03:13,655
using tools you will learn in this course:

38
00:03:13,675 --> 00:03:16,372
Git, Maven, Jenkins, Docker, Kubernetes, and more.

39
00:03:23,892 --> 00:03:26,831
Quick check. Which stage decides what the system must do?

40
00:03:26,851 --> 00:03:29,582
Which stage catches a bad change before customers do?

41
00:03:29,602 --> 00:03:32,021
And which two checks prove an app really works?

42
00:03:40,491 --> 00:03:43,351
Pause the video, answer out loud, then keep going.

43
00:03:46,821 --> 00:03:50,855
Next lesson: requirements, in depth. Subscribe so you do not miss it.
